{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791461360,"nonce":"QmoFHQlmJoV7NhnCa3tirW8XV1o=","title":"HIR: combine guarded scalar reads before Cranelift lowering","message":"Partial escape analysis removes the hot allocations in Lua op_overload, but each field read still introduces a separate virtual/backing-pointer diamond. Consecutive x/y/z reads with the same virtual flag repeat the control flow. The generic metamethod checks and reads of the persistent operands also remain in the loop.\n\nInvestigate combining compatible reads into one guarded region, with scalar fields in the virtual arm and the original loads in the backing-pointer arm. Preserve load order, alias visibility, and effects; do not speculate nullable-pointer loads or remove mutable metatable guards. Assess guarded loop versioning separately if stable metadata can be proved along the fast path.\n\nEvidence: preserving constructor constants in opaque-input scalar slots already removed 45 phis from the new partial-escape HIR (153 to 108). The final NUC LLVM-off operator controls improved long wall time from 209.614 to 96.169 ms and short wall from 48.936 to 44.402 ms. Full control/pinned artifacts are under /tmp/zyntax-partial-final-*-nuc once completed.\n\nARM64 short results are mixed. Latest seven fixed-binary repeats: disabled 50.801 ms wall / 15.930 ms kernel, enabled 53.913 / 17.630. A prior eleven-repeat constant-field comparison had enabled wall 45.983 versus disabled 48.006; keep both records. Long-run gains are consistent. A diagnostic-only phase trace reported 2.35 ms in partial_escape including cleanup. Do not claim short-run success on every architecture.\n\nValidate interpreter-first execution, Cranelift, LLVM, null and opaque inputs, retained aliases, and OSR. Require frozen executables and no Cargo builds during samples. Parent allocation issue: 66b3a58f429b3eb34d360ba9a90764dee4ae4b3a6b2c47400993925c3fc9a68e.","files":null}]}